Dominic Raab: The government cannot apologize for its decision to isolate Spain.
Dominic Raab made an argument in response to the government’s sudden decision to reinstate quarantine measures for Spain.
The ministers announced on Saturday that those who fail to return to Spain and its islands before midnight will have to undergo mandatory self-quarantine for 14 days.
Spain has been on the list of “travel corridors” that can safely travel as set out at Number 10 Downing Street, but recent increases in coronavirus cases have raised public concerns about a second wave of the pandemic.
On Sunday, Foreign Secretary justified the sudden reimposition of travel restrictions by saying it was a "real-time response" to the surge in reported infections in the region.
He said that he had assessed the situation the day before and that they had quickly made a decision.
He added, “We do not have to apologize for this.”
“We must be capable of taking swift and decisive action, especially in cases involving our homeland or Spain or specific countries, and we must act.”
Otherwise, Britain would face another infection risk, triggering a second wave of the pandemic, and have to impose another lockdown.
The Minister of Transport faces mandatory quarantine just like vacationers heading to Spain.
Following two consecutive days of daily reported新增 coronavirus cases exceeding 900 in Spain, the government made this decision.
The Minister of Foreign Affairs acknowledged that many tourists were unable to cancel or change their travel plans in time.
He said, “I know that for those in Spain or considering going to Spain, this decision is extremely unfeeling, but we must have the capacity to take swift and decisive action to protect Britain.”
"We have made notable progress in reducing the case numbers and preventing a resurgence of the epidemic."
Shadow Health Minister Jonathan Ashworth condemned the government’s approach as “chaotic.”
He said the sudden decision at Downing Street left holidaymakers "confused and disappointed."
This Labour Party member told Sophy Ridge, "I understand why they made this decision, but they acted in an uncharacteristic manner by making such a decision in just 24 hours."
Holidaymakers in Spain right now are feeling confused and frustrated, and so are those planning to go on holiday to Spain and the Canary Islands, who don’t know whether their employers will accept them being quarantined for two weeks.
“The government is just saying ‘we hope employers will cooperate.’”
"They made this decision without any discernible pattern in the past 24 hours."
The CDC notes that salmonellosis is a foodborne bacterial disease that annually infects 1.35 million people, hospitalizes 26,500, and results in 420 deaths.
Common symptoms after infection with Salmonella include diarrhea, fever, and stomach cramps, which may begin to appear six hours to six days after exposure to the bacteria.
Symptoms typically last for four to seven days, and most people recover without treatment, though some cases may require hospitalization.
California Dog Park Couple Attacked, Both Because They Were Not Masked During Lunch (Video) – RT News, US News
First mocking with masks, then beating and kicking.
A couple in California were confronted with this when they had lunch with their dog at a dog park. A woman sprayed pepper spray at them because they were not wearing masks while eating.
Ash O'Brien and her husband Jarett Kelley said that they were eating lunch at the Dusty Rhodes Dog Park in San Diego with their three-month-old Beagle puppy on Thursday.
As the couple took off their masks to eat, an "old lady who had suddenly appeared" came over and called them "idiots" because they were not wearing masks, even though they said "how can we eat with masks on."
O’Brien escorted the woman out of the park and stopped her harassment at which point she sprayed them with pepper spray.
The video taken by an onlooker shows that Kelley got sprayed in the face when he was competing with the woman for the spray bottle.
After the attackers left with their dogs, one could hear O’Brien crying in the background.
O'Brien uploaded this video to Facebook and told ABC 10 that he hopes the woman "goes to prison."
California-wide mandates require masks even in fresh-air locations.
People are only allowed to remove their masks during designated times, such as when eating.
However, the state’s guidelines for COVID-19疫情防控 did not mention that bystanders can use non-lethal weapons to enforce this rule.
Even in this “mask shaming” era, it is a crime that carries a $1,000 fine or less than three years in state prison.
The urchin from Chandni Chowk who was flipped over for refusing a bribe to a driver later received a set of apartments and free education worth 100 rupees in a bribe.
It was reported that Thursday, Paras Raykar's car, filled with eggs, was overturned by Indore municipal officials.
The story of a 13-year-old boy who failed to sell eggs and had his car overturned by municipal officials spread on social media, and two days later, the boy from Indore received support from all over the country to help them recover their losses and restart their business.
The boy's family also said that politicians including Nationalist Congress Party leader Rahul Gandhi and Delhi Chief Minister Arvind Kejriwal had reached out to them and offered help as well.
The boy's family also received an apartment as a gift and a promise to cover their children's educational expenses.
It was reported that on Thursday, Paras Raykar’s cart selling eggs was overturned by Indore municipal officials, as the city implemented a staggered opening from left to right measure amid the pandemic.
In a video, a boy says that officials warned him earlier in the day that if he did not pay a bribe of 100 rupees, they would flip his car. The video quickly spread on social media.
The boy says that after refusing to hand over the 100 rupees, those officials overturned his car and broke all the eggs.
The boy also says in the video that due to the impact of the pandemic, daily sales of eggs have declined, and these losses are an additional blow to him.
Since Thursday, the family of the boy has received support from all over the country, with many people providing cash or kind assistance.
The locals also extended a helping hand to this family.
Former Chief Minister Digvijaya Singh funded them and helped the children enroll in a college; People's Party MP Ramesh Mendola also pledged to provide the boy's family with an apartment under the Prime Minister's Awas Yojana housing scheme.
The Indore Press Club also provided food and money for the boy.
The office of Congress Party leader Rahul Gandhi and Delhi Chief Minister Arvind Kejriwal also stepped in.
Paras' grandfather said, "We have received support from many people across the country."
(RP Member) Ramesh Mendola assured us that he would provide us with an apartment under the Prime Minister's Awas Yojana housing scheme.
He provided a bicycle and a资助 of 2,500 rupees.
Digvijaya Singh contributed 10,000 rupees and also promised to look after the education of the two children.
Rahul Gandhi's staff also called us to provide help.
A female staff member of Chief Minister Arvind Kejriwal in Delhi also called us and helped us.
Jyothiraditya Scindia also gave his support.”
The Home Secretary Priti Patel has asked Twitter and Instagram to explain the inappropriate content posted by Wiley.
Due to Twitter and Instagram’s failure to promptly remove inappropriate content posted by Wiley, Priti Patel requested explanations from both platforms.
Police are currently investigating a series of anti-Semitic comments posted by this Grime artist on social media.
The Minister of Home Affairs tweeted on Sunday: "Wiley's anti-Semitic content is deeply offensive."
Twitter and Instagram did not promptly delete these posts, and I have asked them for a full explanation.
Social media companies must act faster to remove these shocking hate speech from their platforms.”
He criticized Twitter for "ignoring antisemitism" because Wiley's tweets remained visible 12 hours after they were posted.
Subsequently, the website deleted multiple tweets and banned the account of the 41-year-old singer for seven days.
Twitter previously stated that it had temporarily suspended Wiley’s account “due to violations of our hate speech policy,” while Facebook said that “hate speech is not allowed on Instagram.”
On Saturday, the NYPD said, "The NYPD is extremely concerned about all reports of anti-Semitism. We are currently evaluating the relevant materials."
Meanwhile, Wiley’s A-List agency has severed all ties with the celebrity.
John Woolf, the former agent of the singer, wrote on Twitter: "Given the anti-Semitic content Wiley published today, we @A_ListMGMT have severed all ties with him. There is no place for anti-Semitism in this society."
Earlier, he had indicated that he neither supported nor condoned Wiley’s comments but would engage with him privately to “educate” him.
However, an Instagram video appears to show Wiley severing ties with his management company.
Other lawmakers criticized Twitter for failing to remove the posts promptly.
Labour Member of Parliament Jess Phillips said on Saturday: "I just looked at what Wiley published. Why is Twitter allowing such blatant anti-Semitism and hate content to remain? It involves dangerous views: Jews have things you don't have, they control everything, they think they're superior, and so on. Such content is dangerous and should be deleted."
Neil Coyle, the Member of Parliament, added, “It seems his management company reacted faster than Twitter did. I want to reiterate that we need legislation (including the Online Harms Bill) to ensure that social media platforms cannot be used to spread hate.”
Government anti-Semitic independent adviser John Mann called the tweets "a precise reflection of why legal changes are necessary to hold people accountable."
The campaign to combat anti-Semitism also called for police to investigate Wiley’s content and called for the closure of his social media accounts “to prevent another spewing of anti-Semitic venom.”
Wiley, whose real name is Richard Cowie, is often referred to as the "Grime Music Father" and was awarded an MBE in 2018 for his contributions to music.
Kudlow says a next coronavirus relief package will include more stimulus checks and extend eviction moratoriums.
White House economic adviser Larry Kudlow promised on Sunday that the federal government would extend the moratorium on evictions and distribute another round of stimulus checks according to the next coronavirus relief plan.
Before Kudlow made his pledge, the White House’s proposal to cut payroll taxes had been rejected by Congress.
The residents of Massart Mallawi village refused to comment on the incident and only offered condolences to the families of the children.
National Women and Children’s Committee advisor Khalid Darwish said in October that approximately 93% of Egyptian children between the ages of 1 and 14 have suffered from violence.
He also noted that prosecution is one of the most important means to eliminate child abuse, and stressed the need for enhanced cooperation between the Committee and the public prosecutors in this regard.
A study by the National Committee on Women, Children, and Families found that children can also suffer from violence in places that should be relatively safe, such as their homes, schools, or clubs, where perpetrators include those who are supposed to care for them – such as parents or teachers.
Reuters criticized White House Press Secretary Kayleigh McEnany for her "political" briefing, but reporters keep asking political questions – RT TV on American news.
AP criticizes Press Secretary Kayleigh McEnany of the White House for making the press conference more political than newsworthy, which worries the media, but apparently forgets that it was the journalists who chose those questions.
The largest US news agency, the Associated Press, published an article on Saturday stating that the White House press conference "may have broken tradition" because McEnany turned the press conference into a "short and flashy campaign to promote and attack the media for the president's re-election," while there is "a relative lack of information about what the government has done."
The last review is understandable as very little new information was disclosed at the press conference.
What the Associated Press does not mention is that virtually no one asked for any new information at the press conference.
Journalists often use White House press officers to draw attention to themselves, and Jim Acosta of CNN took this to an extreme.
They transcend the typical adversarial relationship between the media and government, repeatedly asking loaded questions to doubt President Donald Trump.
In some cases, they choose to become the news themselves, such as when CBS reporter Weijia Jiang made an unambiguous implication in response to President Trump’s retort of “ask China”: “Mr. Xian, why did you address this to me specifically?”
The July 6th launch event is a good example that demonstrates it is no longer a reliable source of accurate information for American citizens.
On that morning, Trump tweeted that NASCAR ratings were down because false reporting about so-called "hate crimes" against driver Bubba Wallace had led to a decision to ban Confederate flags from being displayed during events.
Just as expected, reporters one after another asked McEnany questions related to this tweet, trying to get her to publicly admit that Trump supports the Confederate flag.
McEnany did not allow the reporters to get away with it, as that would have damaged Trump’s interests, but she repeatedly responded that the president has no position on the Confederate flag, and his tweets center on mischaracterizing NASCAR fans as racists.
AP called her remarks during the press conference “outraged.”
The Associated Press story echoes a recent column by NBC News correspondent Jonathan Karl, who is also the president of the White House Correspondents' Association, that appeared in The Washington Post.
Karl says, "Denying reality and using the White House lectern for purely political purposes goes against public trust."
McEnany assumed the position of press secretary in April and hosted her first press conference on May 1st, showcasing her wit and combative nature, which earned her hero status among some Trump supporters.
Before she was appointed, the position of press officer had been vacant for over a year.
Like other aspects of Washington's political life, the relationship between Trump-era White House press secretary and the media underwent significant changes.
This change came from both sides, having already occurred before McEnany's arrival.
At the White House Correspondents' Dinner in April 2018, comedian Michelle Wolf subjected former Press Secretary Sarah Huckabee Sanders to vicious questioning, even MSNBC host Mika Brzezinski called it "shocking."
AP White House correspondent Jill Colvin asked McEnany at her first press conference, "Can you promise never to lie to us from that podium?"
Fox News political analyst Brit Hume, a seasoned journalist with over 50 years of experience, who served as ABC's chief White House correspondent from 1989 to 1996, says that reporters now exhibit "aggressive behavior" during press conferences because they look down on Trump.
He told Tucker Carlson Tonight on Fox News this week that it’s because the journalists are skewing their focus towards being oppositional to Trump and ignoring policy news gathering.
"This is not just a game of cat and mouse," Hume said in discussing the media's intended purpose.
Our goal is not just to bring down government officials but to obtain information.
California protesters burn a courthouse
Oakland, California – Oakland police said that late Saturday night, peaceful protests in California turned into violent incidents. Protesters set fire to the courthouse, smashed police stations, and attacked several police officers.
The Auckland authorities stated on Twitter that protesters smashed windows, sprayed graffiti, and threw fireworks and lasers at police personnel.
Some called for peace on Twitter, with tweets requesting organizers to "help us provide safe spaces and locations for the protesters."
Protests began on Saturday night, with organizations involved including "Moments Wall" (an organization similar to those in Portland, Oregon), among others. At the same time, protesters confronted police officers deployed by the authorities to secure the federal court.
Since the May 25 incident in Minneapolis, where George Floyd was killed, President Donald Trump has sent federal police to suppress protests every night.
Initially, protesters held peaceful demonstrations in Auckland, but they later turned into violent clashes in the evening.
At around 11:30 PM, the police declared the protest an "illegal assembly" and demanded that the demonstrators disperse.
According to news reports, a fire broke out at the Alameda County Superior Court, but the fire was brought under control in a short time.
Police said the protesters initially "cracked windows and made racist slurs against residents."
In photos posted by相关 departments on Twitter, the glass of the police station building has been broken, and walls are also covered with graffiti.
UN senior officials canceled their visit to Pakistan due to flight reasons.
Islamabad (AP) – The newly elected president of the UN General Assembly announced on Sunday that his visit to Pakistan had been postponed “due to technical difficulties with flights.”
Last month, Turkish diplomat Volkan Bozkir successfully became the President of the 75th session of the United Nations General Assembly, which was originally scheduled to arrive in Islamabad on Sunday.
However, he tweeted that his plan to visit the Pakistani capital had been delayed.
Pakistan's Foreign Minister Shah Mahmood Qureshi responded on Twitter to Bozkir by saying, "We warmly welcome you to visit Pakistan soon for a constructive and productive visit."
Just one day before, Qureshi had celebrated the release of his visit, hoping to discuss issues like Kashmir with Bozkir, who serves as the president of Pakistan. Kashmir straddles the border between Pakistan and its neighboring country, India, and both countries claim sovereignty over the entire region.
Since achieving independence from British rule in 1947, there have been three wars between Pakistan and India, both of which involved the disputed region.
Frequent clashes between the two sides in Kashmir result in military and civilian casualties.
Pakistan hopes that the UN resolution will allow people living in the Himalayan region to decide whether to merge with India or Pakistan or become independent, thus pushing for a solution to the Kashmir issue.
Manchester United secured a place in the Champions League, and Paul Pogba hinted at his intention to remain with the team.
After Manchester United locked up their spot in fourth place, Manchester United star Paul Pogba hinted that he would stay with the club this summer.
Securing Leicester City puts Manchester United at third place, ahead of Chelsea under Frank Lampard’s management.
Before 12 more games, Leicester City was still leading Manchester United by 14 points, but the Red Devils made a strong run to reach the quarter-finals.
Pogba has twice requested to leave the team in the past two years, which has led fans to predict that he will depart this summer.
However, the pandemic means that few teams will be able to splash out heavily this summer, and there is no buyer capable of affording both the transfer fee and the wages needed to lure Pogba away from Old Trafford.
Making such a statement has been helpful for Pogba in the past few weeks, as his performance and body language have improved.
After winning the match 2-0, the French player posted on Instagram suggesting that he would stay with the team.
While Pogba may simply be celebrating Juventus’ return to the Champions League, many fans interpret it as him strongly hinting that he will stay at the club to compete against top European players.
Pogba's current contract has one year remaining, but the club can extend it for another year, meaning he would actually be under contract for two more years.
Manchester United hopes to extend Pogba's contract and wants his situation clarified before the next season.
For Pogba, he feels comfortable in the team, and Bruno Fernandes' arrival gives him an extra boost.
This Portuguese midfielder became the heart of Old Trafford, allowing Pogba to advance deeper into the midfield and improve his mood.
Aubameyang helps Arsenal send Watford to英超 relegation
Some players from Watford knelt to the ground, while others turned their heads away and left the field without looking back.
The whistle for Watford’s return to英超 after a fifth year had barely blown, but nobody can say that Watford did not bow out without a fight when victory over Arsenal was needed.
In fact, this was Watford’s most spirited and relentless performance in five years, coming back from a three-point deficit but ultimately losing to Arsenal.
With 16 minutes remaining in the match, Danny Welbeck curled his shot with his heels, and goalkeeper Emiliano Martínez managed to save it. Had he scored, Welbeck would have won a crucial point for Watford, levelled with Arsenal, and secured their spot in the Premier League.
But in the end, Watford failed to achieve this, and their caretaker manager Hayden Mullins stated that if their previous form had been as good as it was in this match, he would not have had to exert himself so much.
Throughout the season, Watford struggled, and owner Pozzo’s management of the team was also chaotic.
A failed campaign may not bring down an empire, but the Pozzo family cannot lay the foundation for Watford to secure its return to the Premier League.
The frequent changes in managers at Watford have become an inescapable fate.
On rough calculation, the next reshuffle will be the 12th in eight years, but the influx of fresh blood from a change of leadership is limited.
Captain Troy Deeney faces relegation with the team, although he could still participate in the UAE Super League.
Some players may choose to leave, such as attacking midfielder Ismaïla Sarr, who again stated that playing in League Two in September is unbelievable, but preserving their strength is currently the top priority.
Arsenal manager Mikel Arteta stated that the unpredictability of the match outcome between the two teams makes the game more exciting.
Arteta also said that Watford’s forwards have been “cheating,” before switching to the footballing term “trying to stay up.”
Arsenal saying this is not without justification; Arsenal is ahead in the score, and Watford must press relentlessly to catch up.
Watford conceded a penalty kick right at the start.
In the first 40 seconds of the game, Craig Dawson accidentally tripped Alexandre Lacazette, and after a lengthy verification by VAR lasting approximately 3 minutes, a penalty was awarded. Pierre-Emerick Aubameyang scored the penalty kick.
Watford press hard, Sarr quickly counter-attacks with the ball on the right side, with Roberto Pereyra and Troy Deeney providing cover as they close in on the goal.
A former Labour Party member told The Daily Mail that even if Starmer expelled Corbyn from the party, they would still bring legal action.
What I mean is that he really brought the Labor Party back into competitiveness.
He has a long way to go in terms of policy, but he has already made an impressive start, having succeeded in re-engaging many who had previously abandoned the Labour Party and no longer considered themselves Labour voters, and viewing him as a serious and earnest person."
Blair indeed fears that the Labour Party is still seen as a party that only raises taxes.
He says, “Based on my experience, if you’re a member of the Labour Party, you need to be very careful with taxation issues, because the public is not sitting there worrying whether you want to raise taxes; they are more likely to worry that you might raise them, so you need to watch out for that mindset.”
The coronavirus will permanently alter the world economy (Opinion)
Similarly to how various security measures became commonplace after the 9/11 events, virus screenings may become a part of our lives as well.
Necessary infrastructure investments to address future viral testing are imperative.
If coronavirus immunity provides only short-term protection, this investment can provide economic safeguards.
During the pandemic, many economies implemented policies similar to Germany’s Kurzarbeit subsidy program.
According to this policy, both the working hours and wages of workers will be reduced, with the shortfall in wages subsidized by the government.
This move maintains the bond between businesses and workers, laying a solid foundation for a swift economic recovery.
Improving the implementation of these policies is also important, which need to become a permanent part of economic recovery tools.
Remote working may become increasingly common.
There is evidence that working from home can be just as efficient as working in an office.
However, many companies are still reluctant to accept the work-from-home model.
Since remote working has been widely tried and proved effective, this approach may continue.
As e-commerce continues to grow, remote medical care, video conferencing, online teaching, and financial technology are increasingly adopted, and the pandemic has accelerated digital transformation.
Companies involved in cross-border supply chains are struggling with various shortages and bottlenecks.
We may see many such companies shifting production back to their home markets.
Finance Minister Fiona Hyslop has said that the pandemic has had a "catastrophic impact" on all activities.
The measures taken to contain the spread of the coronavirus have affected Scottish festive activities and sporting events such as Highland Games.
Ms. Hyslop said, “While we have taken an encouraging step, such as the resumption of drive-in theaters, many industries will still be unable to operate for some time.”
We have engaged in close and constructive collaboration with industry leaders, who have proposed creative solutions for how to operate safely given their respective challenges.
In addition to direct funding, some of the funds will be used to promote innovation and sustain long-term recovery of the event activities.”
The Event Industry Advisory Group notes that the industry was among the first to be restricted by lockdowns and will likely also be one of the last to see widespread easing of restrictions.
Group Chairman Peter Duthie, who also holds the position of CEO of the Scottish Financial Services Association, stated that the gaming events generate nearly £6 billion annually for Scotland.
He said, “It is necessary for the government to allocate additional funds to the industry, which has received widespread support from event organizers and numerous related supply chain enterprises that have suffered severe economic losses during prolonged lockdowns.”
Shivraj Singh Chouhan Confirmed Infected with COVID-19
Central India Chief Minister Shivraj Singh Chouhan confirmed Saturday that his coronavirus test results were positive. In a tweet he posted on Sunday morning, he said he was "okay," and praised the frontline workers fighting the pandemic.
The chief minister reiterated his warning from yesterday, urging everyone to follow防疫guidelines, such as maintaining social distance and wearing masks.
"Friends, I am fine. The frontline fighters risk their lives for others and deserve to be praised. I pay my respects to all the frontline fighters across the country," Chowhan wrote on Twitter from Chirayu Hospital in Bhopal, where he was admitted yesterday.
“Keeping a distance of two meters, washing hands frequently, and wearing masks – these are the most effective weapons we have against the coronavirus,” he said, “I hope everyone can use these weapons to protect themselves and their families.”
Zhouhan concluded with, “Be careful, everyone. I hope you are all safe and healthy. This is also what I pray to God for.”
On Saturday, Chouhan said on Twitter that his virus test came back positive, despite having made every effort to avoid this outcome. He warned, "Even a little carelessness could lead to contracting the coronavirus."
This morning, Chouhan said on Twitter that although the threat of infection remains, there is "no need to be afraid," and called for people with symptoms to get tested.
He says, “If you get infected with the virus, don’t be afraid. Don’t hide your symptoms; inform the doctor immediately and start treatment. Early treatment can lead to recovery.”
Central Bing now has over 27,000 confirmed cases, with 7,600 active cases, and 799 deaths related to the coronavirus.
Over the past few weeks, India has seen a surge in confirmed cases, with daily新增cases reaching up to 45,000 since July 23.
The total number of confirmed cases of COVID-19 is rapidly approaching 1.4 million, with over 467,000 active cases and over 32,000 deaths linked to the virus.
And, for studying rocks on Mars, what could be more suitable than a rock from Mars itself? says Professor Caroline Smith, curator of the Meteorite Gallery at the Natural History Museum.
Smith is also a member of the science team for the 2020 Mars mission, and he adds that scientists believe the rocks they bring back came from Mars.
Micro-bubbles within the meteorite are identical to the composition of Mars' atmosphere, which is why we are certain that this rock came from there.
It is believed that this Martian meteorite was formed when a small asteroid or comet collided with Mars about 60 to 70 million years ago, scattering fragments into space.
One rock swept through the solar system before landing on Earth.
This meteorite, now known as SAU 008, was discovered in Oman in 1999 and has been kept by the Natural History Museum since then.
On the instruments aboard the "Perseverance" rover, there is a high-precision laser instrument named SherLOC, which will be used to decipher the chemical composition of Martian rocks and determine whether they contain organic matter that could indicate the presence or continued existence of life on Mars.
Adding a SAU 008 block ensures maximum precision for this operation.
Smith added, “We selected this rock particularly because its chemical composition matches and it is an extremely hard rock.”
One of the Martian meteorites we found is very fragile, but this one is as hard as an old boot.
When the InSight finds valuable rocks, it will store them on the surface of Mars.
Then, these rocks will be retrieved by subsequent robotic missions sent to Mars and launched back into Earth for research and analysis.
A man was shot near Mambouin, nearby Verrières.
Last night, a man was shot in the western suburbs of Melbourne and was urgently taken to the hospital for treatment.
Police arrived at Mambourin, near Vireby, around 8 PM after receiving an alarm. They found a 20-year-old man with multiple gunshot wounds to his lower body on Yard Street.
The man was taken to the hospital for treatment and is reportedly in good condition with no life-threatening injuries.
The police are investigating this case.
Oliver Spencer's Sunday: "I don't dress up on Sundays; I wear loungewear."
How do you usually start your Sundays?
Tripping over a dog, I stumbled down the stairs on my way to brew tea for my wife.
After getting off the ferry, we drove onto A3 Highway and headed back to London to prepare for the start of the workday. Upon arriving home, the children went to bed, and after I walked my dog, I had another bowl of cereal.
I would be happy to return from Whiter Island to London, as I know that without doing so, I wouldn’t have a home on Whiter Island.
Kenyan police in Garissa arrested for shooting incident
Two Kenyan police officers have been arrested for allegedly causing two deaths in a shooting incident.
The shooting occurred on Saturday at the Soko Ng’ombey market in eastern Garissa County, where protests were ongoing over an alleged murder case happening inside the market.
Two victims are named Aden Abdi Madobe and Muhiyadin Adow Shibin.
The Kenyan Police Service ombudsman has launched an investigation into the shooting incident.
The investigation process includes forensic analysis of the involved firearms.
Aden Duale, a Garissa lawmaker, called the incident "heinous" and condemned "police brutality" in a post on Facebook.
Several activists have also expressed concerns about police enforcement actions during the pandemic.
According to data from Amnesty International, since March, Kenyan police have caused 21 deaths, all due to non-compliance with curfew orders and mask-wearing requirements for防疫 measures.
At the beginning of this month, police in the capital city of Nairobi released tear gas and arrested several protesters participating in the annual Saba Saba march – which stems from protests 30 years ago opposing former President Daniel arap Moi's dictatorial rule.
A reporter in front notes that the protests this year have become more radical due to the anger sparked by police crackdowns during the pandemic lockdowns.
Enhanced security at the U.S. Consulate General in Chengdu, China
On Sunday, security at the U.S. Consulate General in Chengdu, China was upgraded, and U.S. personnel were preparing to evacuate.
Three moving trucks entered the building, with uniformed police and plainclothes police lined up along both sides of the street, and metal barriers set up along the roadside.
China's Global Times editor posted a tweet stating that the US Consulate General in Chengdu was required to close within 72 hours, i.e., at 10 am on Monday, July 27th (0200 GMT).
China accuses some personnel of the US Consulate General in Chengdu of engaging in activities inconsistent with their status.
China’s Foreign Ministry Spokesperson Wang Wenbin did not provide any details but said that such activities interfere with China's internal affairs and harm China's national security interests.
China demanded on Friday that the US consulate in Chengdu be closed as a countermeasure to the US's demand to close China's consulate in Houston, Texas.
Washington demands that China close the Consulate General in Houston “in order to protect American intellectual property and the personal information of Americans.”
After the Chinese Consulate General in Houston closed on Friday, a group of individuals resembling American officials entered the facility through the back door.
Wang Wenbin said on Saturday that this action seriously violates international law and bilateral agreements, and China will make a response, but did not specify the measures.
Closing embassies head-on further exacerbated tensions between the two countries over trade and industrial espionage, as well as human rights issues.
Vandals provide wrecking tools, and apes at the zoo swing various implements.
The Northside Wildlife Sanctuary in Mercer County is notorious for its primates, which attack cars parked inside the fence, breaking windshields or rearview mirrors.
Some saw baboons at the North Wales Wildlife Park in Merseyside wielding knives, screwdrivers, even chainsaws.
Staff is worried that pranksters might arm these annoying primates, which could then charge at stopped cars and peel windshields or rearview mirrors.
Now, staff are worried that visitors may give them lethal weapons as “jokes,” which is increasing their aggression, and some have even seen the monkeys rifling through toolboxes.
A staff member of a园区 told The Sunday Times, "We are not sure whether tourists who wanted to see them attack cars provided them with weapons, or whether they stole them from pickup trucks and cargo vans themselves."
"They do indeed rummage through others' toolboxes and carry them around. One has seen a chimpanzee dragging a chainsaw."
Another staff member said, “We have seen baboons carrying small knives and screwdrivers. I was wondering if tourists were giving them to the baboons.”
Chimpanzees attacks were visible, even to the repairmen in northwest England who knew about this zoo.
A mechanic from Manchester Salford said that people knew what was happening at the zoo, and two of his clients were recently victims of baboon attacks this year.
They said that one minute the children wanted to see the monkeys getting into the cars, and the next minute, the license plates were gone, but such problems have brought good business to repair shops.
Northside Zoo insists that it is just as safe as a McDonald’s drive-thru, as long as visitors stay in their vehicles during a 5-mile wildlife tour of the park. The zoo reopened on June 15th, taking safety measures to address the pandemic.
This zoo covers about 222.7 hectares and houses animals such as lions, tigers, rhinos, zebras, and camels, dismissing reports of them having weapons for baboons.
A spokesperson said, “We believe many such stories have been exaggerated through hearsay and embellished along the way, leading to some discoveries in the park seeming unbelievable.”
In 2012, car manufacturer Hyundai placed its new model i30 car in the zoo with 40 monkeys for ten hours to prove the vehicle's strength is sufficient to ensure the safety of families with children.
Manchester United and Chelsea entered the UEFA Champions League, while Brighton and Hove Albion and Watford dropped out of the English Premier League.
On Sunday, Manchester United and Chelsea advanced to the UEFA Champions League by defeating Leicester City in the final day of the Premier League, while Brighton and Hove Albion and Watford were relegated, ending their five-year peak battle.
After Bruno Fernandes and Jesse Lingard scored, Manchester United defeated Leicester City 2-0, securing third place for Ole Gunnar Solskjaer’s team.
Chelsea defeated Wolves 2-0, thanks to goals and assists from Mason Mount, securing fourth place and overtaking Leicester City (which had been consistently in the top four during the season) to claim the final spot in the UEFA Champions League.
Leicester City will participate in the European league, and so will Tottenham, which drew 1-1 with Wolves in Crystal Palace and edged out Wolves due to a better goal difference.
With the play-off for the remaining two relegation spots coming down to the final moments, Aston Villa scored in the 84th minute, only to be equalized one minute later by West Ham, leaving them level at 1-1.
Captain Jack Grealish scored a goal for Aston Villa, which may be his last match for his childhood club.
Bournemouth defeated Everton 3-1 but finished one point behind Aston Villa.
Watford were relegated as the second-from-last team after losing 3–2 to Arsenal.
Manchester United scored over 100 goals during the league season and won 5-0 against relegated Norwich, securing their place in the Champions League. This match was also David Silva's final Premier League game, which he had been playing for 10 years.
Kevin De Bruyne set a record this season with 20 assists and two goals.
Liverpool defeated Newcastle United 3-1, ending their title quest for this season, with the club finishing with a score of 99 points.
Additionally, Southampton defeated Sheffield United 3-1 this season, with Danny Ings scoring 22 goals this season.
And Leicester City player Jamie Vardy scored the most goals and won the Golden Boot.
Gwyneth Paltrow hopes to instill good "values" in her children.
Gwyneth Paltrow says she aims to educate her children “not just by giving them fish,” but rather to instill in them good values.
Paltrow has two children with ex-husband Chris Martin, ages 16 (Apple) and 14 (Moses), and she hopes they will develop good values rather than relying on their parents' fame.
"Why, among other things, I believe it is important to cultivate a child’s ethical character and ensure that their values align with either your own values or their own sense of identity," she told Rob Lowe in the podcast.
What actually helps is pointing you in the right direction, but you won’t get anywhere if you just try like everyone else.
She adds, “As parents, what I can do for my children is teach them what is right from wrong and what work is. I indeed tried to avoid just handing them fish.”
Gwyneth Paltrow also took steps to keep her children’s lives as private as possible, ensuring the privacy of their social media accounts.
Considering there were still upcoming elections, the Renewal Party ultimately agreed to join the coalition government.
Ted Cruz calls Pelosi's approach "using money to solve problems and putting the US government on the brink of a shutdown"
WASHINGTON – Texas Republican Senator Ted Cruz criticized House Speaker Nancy Pelosi and the Democratic Caucus for the coronavirus relief package they have been pushing, accusing it of aiming “to solve problems with money and shut down America,” and negotiations over subsequent measures continue.
Cruz said on Sunday during an interview with Face the Nation that the US is facing two simultaneous crises: a global pandemic that has taken more than 600,000 lives worldwide, and an "absolute economic disaster."
Cruz criticized Pelosi for “not addressing any of them” during an interview with “Forbes,” before he agreed to it.
Cruz said that her goal was to solve problems with money and prevent a government shutdown in the US.
You should especially look at the $3 trillion bill she is trying to push, which is nothing more than spending money without actually solving the problem. Our goal should be to get Americans back to work so they can support their families.
Republican senators and the White House last week negotiated the terms of a fourth-phase response to the pandemic, which is expected to amount to $1 trillion.
Last weekend, Chief of Staff Mark Meadows and Treasury Secretary Steven Mnuchin returned to Capitol Hill to further discuss with Senate staff. On Sunday, Mnuchin, in an interview with Fox News Channel, stated that Majority Leader Mitch McConnell of the Senate would unveil the details on Monday.
The most immediate issue facing lawmakers and the focus of the debate between Republicans and Democrats is that extended unemployment benefits have expired and will be canceled this weekend.
The Democrats want the “Phase IV” coronavirus relief package to continue including the additional $600 in benefits, while Republicans call for cutting this amount, arguing that some Americans receive unemployment income that exceeds their wages.
Cruz says that currently half or more Americans receiving increased unemployment benefits earn more than they did when working. He notes that some small business owners have encountered difficulties hiring due to this, as he learned from speaking with small business owners in Texas.
He says, “Of course they won’t come back to work, because the federal government is paying them sometimes twice their income.”
Cruz believes that Congress should focus on passing a recovery bill that lowers taxes for small businesses and removes regulations in order to get Americans back to work.
He said, “The Recovery Act is not about spending tens of trillions of dollars, but pausing payroll taxes, which amounts to giving a raise to every working American. This is the way that truly gets people back to work.”
President Trump requested in his last relief package that payroll taxes be reduced, but Mnuchin stated that the proposal would not advance through the legislative process because the Republicans do not accept it.
President Trump confirms that the next bill does not include a payroll tax cut, but attempts to blame it on the Democrats.
Last week, Cruz objected to the cost of the new proposal, stating on Sunday that he disagreed with the measure proposed by Senate Republicans.
However, he also criticized the $3 trillion bill passed by the Democratic-controlled House in May, accusing his opponents of deliberately crafting measures that would harm the president’s interests before the November election.
He says: “The Democrats’ sole goal is to defeat Donald Trump, and they naively believe that shutting down America’s government and closing every school in the country is the best way to do so.”
Prime Minister Narendra Modi mourns the Kargil heroes and says Pakistan is trying to stab India in the back.
Today is the 21st anniversary of the Kargil War, and Indian Prime Minister Narendra Modi, while paying tribute to military martyrs, stated that despite India's efforts to maintain friendly relations, Pakistan continues to try to stab India in the back.
Prime Minister Modi said during his monthly radio program "Mann ki Baat" that "Pakistan caused this disaster; they devised cunning plans to grab Indian land and divert their domestic conflicts."
He said, “Pakistan is shameless for trying to occupy Indian territory and diverting public attention from its internal turmoil.”
At the time, India was working to repair its relationship with Pakistan.
But, as the essence of evil is to fight with everyone for no reason.
Such people will only do evil, even if the other party wants to do them a good deed."
He said, “That is why Pakistan chose to ignore India’s efforts to maintain the relationship and stabbed it in the back.”
He added, “Imagine the enemy entrenched atop high hills, while our troops fight below them. But our high spirits and true strength helped us defeat the enemy on the mountains.”
"Our brave military showcased India's tremendous power in the Kargil War," Prime Minister Modi said, recalling what he had seen and experienced during his visit to the war zone.
India celebrates an event annually to commemorate the Kargil victory, marking the day in 1999 when Indian Army recaptured all of India's outposts in the Ladakh region that were occupied by Pakistani forces almost 21 years ago.
On July 26, 1999, the Indian Army declared "Vijay Operation" a resounding success.
The war that took place on the snowy high grounds of Ladakh region lasted for nearly three months and resulted in the death of over 500 Indian soldiers.
Earlier today, several high officials and military leaders also paid respects at the "Unknown Soldier Memorial" (Amar Jawan Jyoti) at the National War Memorial, including Defence Minister Rajnath Singh, Deputy Defence Minister Shripad Naik, Chief of Staff of the Indian Army Bipin Rawat, Chief of Army Staff M M Naravane, Chief of Air Staff R K S Bhadauria, and Admiral Karambir Singh, the Chief of Naval Staff.
India and Pakistan’s relations have been tense, marked by a series of terrorist attacks, for decades after the Kargil War.
India insists that negotiations and terrorist attacks cannot occur simultaneously, and Pakistan needs to dismantle all terrorist camps operating on its territory.
Online lending firms issue a warning: Small and medium-sized enterprise loan schemes may not achieve their goals.
Small enterprises are increasingly turning to online lending institutions to meet their funding needs, as they rarely attract the attention of large banks.
However, Mr. Callaghan noted that peer-to-peer lenders do not have an advantage over large banks, as they cannot access the same cheap funding, and he also warned that the small and medium-sized enterprise guarantee scheme is unpopular.
He said, “I believe this proposal is unlikely to achieve its goal. People call us expecting cheap funding from us, rather than from the government covering half of the loan amount.”
So, after we called them, they were very disappointed because someone had expressed a desire for a discounted price, but our financing costs remained unchanged.
Mr. Callaghan added that such a solution would be “a walk in the park” if fintech companies were able to secure cheaper financing and then transfer those funds to small businesses.
This is somewhat of a dilemma – large banks can obtain funds at lower costs, but they prefer to avoid risks, so they do not lend in the same way. They will not access data unless there is collateral, in which case they feel secure. Otherwise, they would be uncomfortable, ” he said. “Unless we can get cheaper funding, I don’t think the market has any illusions about the popularity of this solution.”
Moula in Melbourne also participated in the formulation of the plan, CEO Aris Allegos stated that the 50% guarantee yield suggested in the government scheme should be transferred to small businesses, but different lending institutions would handle it differently.
He said, "I can understand that others may not have experienced such a cost structure."
This may involve their funding structure, as well as whom they lend to and how they assess the risk of these categories.
Dr. Allegos declined to disclose exactly how much financing Moulah had disbursed under the original plan, but stated that the revised plan "concentrates on reflecting the necessary changes," thereby providing greater flexibility for both parties involved in the loan.
But he is very concerned about the “two-speed economy” that is running and its impact on small businesses.
He says that for small businesses, the biggest challenge faced by Moula and loan providers is what they call the "cliff" and how to manage it.
Overall, while there has been some growth in demand, it has not yet returned to pre-pandemic levels.
The online lending institution Prospa, listed on the Australian Securities Exchange, also refrains from disclosing the amount of loans it has disbursed under the scheme, citing this as sensitive commercial information. However, CEO Greg Moshal noted that the government’s expansion of the scheme has been well received.
He said, “We support various enhancements to the solution that enable small businesses to access a range of financing products tailored to their needs, including flexible short-term cash flow products.”
Virginia Beach拆除南部同盟纪念碑
Virginia Beach (Virginia) – On Sunday, the city of Virginia Beach removed a Confederate monument that is 115 years old from its public square. Two days prior, the city council had unanimously voted to remove it.
According to media reports, workers dismantled the 27-foot (8.2-meter) tall monument on Saturday morning.
The monument was erected in front of the former Princess Anne County Courthouse in 1905, where slave auctions also took place.
On Thursday evening, the city council unanimously decided to proceed with the related demolition work.
The city council heard from several speakers who viewed the monument as a source of division for many and constantly bringing to mind painful pasts.
Given recent protests and destruction of other monuments, the monument is currently completely covered and surrounded by locked gates.
The monument will be temporarily retained.
The city now needs to wait for a response from historical organizations or other relevant entities willing to handle this monument.
Protests continue, with police attempting to use new less-lethal tools.
In Portland and across the country, violent clashes continue between law enforcement and protesters, with debates raging about how to reduce police use of lethal force.
Some companies are ramping up efforts to provide new high-tech tools like police-controlled handcuffs, as alternatives to more dangerous weapons such as pepper spray, rubber bullets, and flash bangs.
Amnesty International analyzed 500 videos of protests from May 26 to June 5 and found that there were 125 instances of excessive use of force in 40 states and the District of Columbia.
Brian Castner, an Arms Investigator with International Crisis Group, said, “We saw the misuse of tear gas, pepper spray, pepper balls, and 40mm impact rounds, all used inappropriately in combination with various non-lethal weapons.”
Some still hold the view that choice is important for avoiding gun use.
Corey Pegues, a former deputy inspector of the New York Police Department, stated, “We should provide police officers with various personal protective equipment instead of lethal weapons like guns, as policing is the only profession in America where citizens grant the power to take a life.”
There is a new tool called the Bola Wrap, a handheld device that can shoot a 8-foot Kevlar rope to ensnare suspects.
Other new equipment includes long-range acoustic devices (also known as acoustic cannons), directed energy weapons that heat human skin with lasers, new shells made of materials like foam and chalk powder, tear gas, and tasers.
Just days after George Floyd was killed by police, protests turned violent, and the stock price of gun manufacturer Axon surged over 18%, hitting an all-time high.
It is预计 that the market for low-lethality weapons will reach $11.85 billion by 2023.
Watch the video to learn about the police’s newly developed high-tech non-lethal tools, how to regulate their usage, and what risks these tools pose to future policing and communities.
The UN says that Afghanistan is hosting thousands of Pakistani Taliban militants.
Islamabad – A UN report states that over 6,000 Pakistani insurgents are hiding in Afghanistan, with the majority being illegal members of the Pakistani Taliban organization responsible for attacking military and civilian targets in Pakistan.
According to a report released this week, the organization known as the Tehrik-i-Taliban (TTP) has links with the Islamic State's (ISIS) branch in Afghanistan.
Some members of TTP even joined an affiliate of IS located in eastern Afghanistan.
On Sunday, the Afghan government did not respond to an Associated Press request for comment on related issues.
Reports indicate that ISIS-K (Islamic State in Khorasan Province) in Afghanistan has been severely hit by Afghan security forces and US and NATO forces, sometimes also facing attacks from the Taliban in Afghanistan.
The report was written by a team supporting analysis and sanctions monitoring, which tracks terrorist organizations around the world.
The report estimates that there are around 2,200 members of the IS group in Afghanistan, despite its leadership being weakened, which still considers a Syrian citizen, Abu Said Mohammad al-Khorasani, one of its leaders.
The report also notes that monitoring teams reported that two senior ISIS commanders, Abu Qutaibah and Abu Hajar al-Iraqi, had arrived in Afghanistan from the Middle East.
The report notes that although ISIS-held territory is shrinking, they still have the capability to carry out attacks of some scale in various regions of the country, including Kabul.
The group also seeks to win over Taliban fighters who oppose signing an agreement with the United States, referring to the peace agreement that was signed between the US and the Taliban in February.
The aim of this agreement is to allow the United States to end its 19-year-long war in Afghanistan and to hope that the Taliban will guarantee that their territory will not be used by terrorist organizations.
It is expected that the agreement will also ensure that the Taliban fully commit to fighting IS.
The second part of the agreement – and perhaps the most important part – calls for talks between Taliban leaders and political representatives in Kabul.
Later that Saturday, the U.S. State Department issued a statement saying that its peace envoy Zalmay Khalilzad was still busy in the region, trying to push stalled negotiations that have been repeatedly delayed due to disagreements over prisoner-release plans.
This US-backed agreement calls for the release of 5,000 Taliban prisoners by the Afghan government in return for 1,000 government and military personnel being released by the Taliban as a gesture of good faith prior to negotiations.
Currently, the Afghan government is refusing to release approximately 600 Taliban prisoners, labeling them as high-profile criminals, but is proposing to release the remaining prisoners.
The Taliban rejected the government's proposal.
"This is an opportunity for both sides to engage in the most direct internal negotiations yet, and a critical step towards ending the 40-year-long Afghan war," the U.S. State Department stated.
"While important progress has been made in exchanging prisoners, further efforts are needed to solve the problem."
Earlier this week, the Taliban’s political spokesman said that the Taliban plan to hold talks with Kabul’s political leaders after Eid al-Adha (an Islamic holiday) next month, provided that the prisoner release work is completed first.
The biggest concern for Pakistan is the militants within Afghanistan, particularly those linked to the TTP, Jamaat-ul-Ahrar, and Lashkar-e-Islam, or the Balochistan Liberation Army, which launched large-scale attacks in southern Sindh and southwestern Balochistan that month.
Multiple Pakistani military personnel were killed in clashes with militants in southwestern Balochistan.
TTP organization conducted a highly terroristic attack on Pakistan in 2014, resulting in the death of 140 members of the Pakistani Army College.
Many of them are students, some as young as 5 years old.
The report states that “foreign terrorist forces operating in Afghanistan pose a threat to the security of both countries, with an estimated number ranging from 6,000 to 6,500 people, of which the majority are members of the TTP.”
HSBC denies setting up a trap for Chinese company Huawei in US probe – RT Business News
HSBC, headquartered in London, through social media stated that the bank did not fabricate evidence as reported by Chinese media to target Huawei, leading to the arrest of Huawei’s CFO in Canada.
A now-unavailable WeChat post stated that banks claimed their actions did not trigger an investigation by the US into Huawei, the world’s largest supplier of telecom network equipment, as scrutiny into Huawei’s alleged violation of US sanctions against Iran on Iran had already begun well before the banks got involved, in late 2016.
HSBC stated on Saturday that it has no hostile intent towards Huawei and there is no "frame-up" of Huawei, and added that it will never distort any facts about its customers.
To respond to the information request from the U.S. Department of Justice (US DOJ), HSBC Group merely stated objective facts.
HSBC neither ‘fabricated’ evidence nor ‘covered up’ the facts.”
The platform blocked this article, citing it as “in violation of rules,” and the content is no longer visible.
Following criticism of HSBC allegedly colluding with Washington to set up traps for Huawei by multiple Chinese media outlets, including state-backed newspapers, the bank quickly issued this clarification.
In December 2018, Canadian authorities arrested Huawei's Chief Financial Officer Meng Wanzhou at the request of US agencies, and for the following eighteen months, the company remained a focal point in diplomatic news.
Ma Xiaohu was charged with fraud for allegedly using shell companies to violate sanctions against Iran and failing to disclose the relationship between Huawei and Iranian company Skycom to banks.
The new evidence cited by The Global Times shows that prior to this, HSBC was very aware of the relationship between the Chinese company and Skycom.
It is reported that the bank chose to “collude” with US authorities in order to avoid harsher penalties imposed in 2012, when HSBC had already been forced to admit to money laundering and violations of sanctions, including those related to Iran.
At that time, the bank was fined approximately $1.9 billion and was required to take measures to prevent similar misconduct.
In an article on the matter, the newspaper wrote: “There is evidence that HSBC was aware of this relationship but deliberately sold its clients’ confidential business documents to the US side in order to let the US government overlook the British bank’s improper behavior and avoid the penalties it should have faced under the Deferred Prosecution Agreement of 2012.”
This Chinese newspaper, under government control, adds in an open warning that if such collusion with banks is confirmed, it may impose "severe punishment" on them, in China (one of HSBC's main markets).
In recent weeks, this well-known bank has been criticized multiple times.
Last month, HSBC was criticized for supporting the new national security law implemented in Hong Kong, where the bank originated and contributed more than half of its profits.
HSBC finds itself in an awkward position: US Secretary of State Mike Pompeo calls it “bowing and scraping” in the West; while in the East, Chinese media hail its “late but supportive” stance.
Angola: Pedalé Hospital transferred under the supervision of the Ministry of Health
Rwanda – Republic President João Lourenço transferred on Friday the oversight of the Pedro Maria Tonha Pedalé Hospital under construction in Rwanда to the Ministry of Health.
The Office of the President of the Republic stated in a press release that this move is aimed at implementing the government's construction principles, expanding the network of hospitals, and ensuring better medical and pharmaceutical services for the public.
The document also notes that "in the current exceptional circumstances, this measure allows the Angolan people to have a differentiated reference hospital with modern technology and precision instruments for diagnosing and treating complex diseases."
The article notes that President of Angola’s decision also takes into account the country's population growth, which has fueled a “huge demand” for the most diverse range of high-quality healthcare services.
The document also notes that this demand is reasonable, as hospitals encompass complex disease treatments, teaching, and research related to ophthalmology and maternal and child health, areas for which strategies and management should fall under the purview of the Ministry of Health.
Algeria: President Tebboune mourns Mahmoud Ghannouchi – Algeria has lost a patriot and a steadfast fighter.
Algiers – President of the Republic Abdelmadjid Tebboune paid tribute to late National Assembly Member Mujahed Mahmoud Guemama, who passed away on Friday, describing Algeria as having “lost a patriot and a warrior who devoted himself to the cause of the people and the nation.”
President Tebboune wrote in the condolence letter: "Today, Mujahed Mahmoud Guemama has left us. Algeria has lost a patriot, a fighter who dedicated himself to the nation's cause alongside the people, who did not waver from the moment he took up arms. His resolve was unwavering, and he fought side by side with valiant mujahideen brothers until he took his last breath on this day of profound sorrow, and we could only listen to the will of God."
"Late Mahmoud Guemama, may he rest in peace, came from a special generation of believers who sacrificed themselves for their country as patriotic fighters."
"In 1960, they joined the Tahart training camp of Wilaya VI at the height of their lives, joining the ranks of the revolution and freeing Algeria from the claws of inferior colonialism, sacrificing their lives."
The late leader was battle-hardened in building a united and prosperous Algeria, and his name became known to all.
The President added: “In the far south, Mahmoud Guemama is a revered mediator. Once any sparks of discord are ignited, he steps in to help his compatriots reach reconciliation.”
These qualities and values earned Mahmoud Guemama the respect and trust of the people of Tamanrasset, leading to his election as a member of the National People's Assembly for multiple terms, representing their concerns and will, and speaking on behalf of all Algerian people.
None can pay tribute sufficiently to Mujahed Mahmoud Guemama, may he rest in peace.
"Today, he left us at the most critical moment when Algeria needed him," said the President.
Finally, President Tebboune said, “I pray to the Almighty God that the deceased may be bathed in the sanctity and mercy of your light, welcoming his arrival in the boundless heaven alongside the shuhada and the pious.”
I pray that Allah gives his family and loved ones patience and comfort… Only you have such supreme power.”
Travel Guide for COVID-19: Countries Implementing Entry Restrictions for Bulgarian Passengers
More countries are implementing travel restrictions. Starting this week, the Czech Republic hopes to conduct coronavirus tests on seasonal workers from Bulgaria. Denmark requires that only Bulgarian citizens who have registered their residence, hold employment contracts, or possess proof of business travel can enter the country.
Starting today, people who have visited Bulgaria and Romania in the past 14 days must undergo mandatory quarantine in Italy. Finland only accepts travelers with 'justifiable reasons' for entry.
Austria mandates mandatory home quarantine even if PCR tests come back negative. Germany also implements the same policy for arrivals. In Romania’s southern border region, entry is not permitted without sufficient reason for the next 14 days. Greece has taken more stringent measures at its land borders.
The Ministry of Foreign Affairs announced that as of Tuesday, persons arriving in Greece from Bulgaria must present a negative PCR test result to Greek border authorities, with the test needing to be completed within 72 hours prior to entering Greece's southern neighboring country.
They also announced that travelers must present corresponding test certificates to be allowed entry. The certificates must be issued in English and include the three names and number found on the traveler's passport or ID. If the certificates do not meet these requirements, authorities in neighboring southern countries will not accept them.
Passengers need to have RT-PCR tests conducted at the laboratory by collecting nasopharyngeal or oropharyngeal secretions through extraction. Accepted test results include those obtained from national reference laboratories, national health laboratories, or private labs that have been accredited by relevant national certification bodies (not necessarily specifically for COVID-19), after undergoing a test for COVID-19.
Greek citizens, residents in Greece, and travelers with significant professional reasons are exempt from this measure.
The Greek Ministry of Foreign Affairs also announced that passengers arriving in Greece on Tuesday, July 28, and Wednesday, July 29, who did not get tested before their departure will undergo testing upon arrival by Greek authorities.
The following requirements remain in effect: passengers traveling to Greece by air must fill out and generate a QR code for the electronic Passenger Locator Form (PLF) at least 24 hours in advance, and submit the form to Greek authorities upon arrival in Greece. For more information, see travel.gov.gr.
Currently, there have been no changes to requirements for land borders between Bulgaria and Greece. The Ministry of Foreign Affairs explains that "all passengers entering Greece via the land border, regardless of their nationality, must fill out an electronic Passenger Locator Form (PLF) at least 24 hours before arrival and generate a QR code."
The closure of the Ilinden, Kapitan Petko Voyvoda, and Makaza checkpoints has increased traffic flow near the border of Petrich. Crossing is not an issue, and there are no queues of trucks. Everyone is waiting to go sea from Greece. Passengers need to wait about an hour and a half to cross.
Three checkpoints are open at the border between Bulgaria and Greece. Currently, the section between "Ilinden - Exohi" and Ivaylovgrad is closed.
Dominic Cummings visits the SAS base and is taken hostage.
The Prime Minister’s Chief Adviser experienced the feeling of being held hostage during a visit to the base last week.
For some harassed civil servants, the idea of holding Dominic Cummings hostage by pointing a gun at his head might have an appealing twist.
The hardline Prime Minister’s Chief Advisor experienced first-hand the taste of it when he visited SAS headquarters at Sterling Line last week.
The Sunday Mail reported that part of his itinerary on Wednesday included visiting an "indoors counter-terrorism training room," where elite soldiers hone their skills in dealing with terrorists.
He also toured the Hereford base under guidance, inspected the military equipment of the brigade, heard a briefing on recent operations, including efforts to clear Islamic State terrorists from Iraq.
The important visitors to the Stanley防线 were given a special welcome – experiencing the "Close Quarters Combat Pod," where SAS trains its close-combat skills in this two-story building.
Princess Diana也曾 visited here, and in a training session in 1983, her hair accidentally caught fire from the spark of a thrown grenade.
It is known that Mr. Cummings arrived by helicopter, and the base is named after Colonel David Stirling, founder of the regiment.
He also visited the nearby Pontrilas training area, which houses other classified units.
This visit is one stop on his tour of highly classified British security facilities, which has garnered attention from politicians and military leaders.
When news of its plan leaked earlier this month, Conservative Backbencher and Chair of the House of Commons Defence Select Committee Tobias Ellwood said that Members of Parliament should be afforded the same opportunities as ministers and civil servants to question defence and security matters.
The situation of requiring a special advisor appointed by political figures to provide testimony is rare.
According to reports, SAS senior officials used the activities on Wednesday to highlight the importance of maintaining the numbers of the Royal Marines and the Parachute Regiment.
According to earlier reports, units that had previously sent large numbers of personnel to special forces could potentially be downsized or merged to save costs.
A defense source said: "SAS consists of 250 soldiers, half of whom were previously paratroopers. The SBS, on the other hand, is smaller in size; without the support of the Royal Marines, it may be difficult to sustain."
"Cummings was informed that these units must be fully retained."
Mr. Cummings had previously complained that "millions of pounds are continuing to go down the drain in military procurement," and recently attended a meeting at MI5 and MI6 headquarters, and plans to visit the SBS headquarters in Poole, Dorset, and the National Cryptological Laboratory in Broughton Down, Wiltshire.
The Ministry of Defense did not respond to an inquiry for comment last night.
"Sunday Morning": Day Show: A Tribute to Rodgers and Hart
2020 was the centenary of Richard Rodgers and Lorenz Hart's legendary partnership, one of Broadway's most colorful musical duo.
In tribute to these two songwriters, Broadway star Jelani Alladin (from Frozen and Hercules), Christiane Pitts (from Kong and Moana), and dancer Richard Riaz Yoder (from Love, Loss and What I Wear and Shuffle Along) performed a modern adaptation of the show – "The Runaway Girl," which was specially recorded for the audience of Sunday Morning. This adaptation is based on Rodgers and Hart’s original work.
Bassist Russell Hall performs alongside drummer Bryan Carter, who provides the musical direction and arrangement for the show.
California's Larson County wildfire is under control.
Two wildfires broke out in Lyon County, located northeast of Sacramento County in California, with an area burned exceeding 12,141 hectares. Firefighters are still working to contain them.
The California fire department said that as of Saturday morning, the "Gold" wildfire burning in the southern part of the Addy region was only 40% contained, with large numbers of homes and ranches still at risk from the blaze, which could have a major impact on local agriculture, livestock, and forestry sectors.
The fire has been burning for four days, having destroyed approximately 8,498 hectares of land and seven houses, and injuring two firefighters who were taken to hospital.
Firefighters worked overnight to break through and reinforce the fireline under limited conditions.
But according to the weather forecast, it was expected to be windy and low humidity on that day.
The "Hog" wildfire that erupted in western Susanville has destroyed two buildings and approximately 4,047 hectares of land.
The California Department of Forestry and Fire Protection stated that after firefighters worked all night to clear and reinforce the fireline, the fire is now under control.
As of Saturday morning, the wildfire had been raging for six days, with 45% of it brought under control.
Watch out for those seemingly charming gardening schemes.
While it may be an embarrassing confession for a botanist, I am indeed deeply addicted to horticultural tricks.
On the positive side, I tend to think of myself as an open-minded, or at least somewhat irrational optimist, and many things in the plant world are not easily discernible through intuition.
But I do not shy away from admitting that I have fallen into this gardening trick countless times.
While I believe in the value of trying new things, I avoid some of the most common gardening traps that I've encountered, which aren't particularly appealing even if one is not inclined to go through "hell mode."
Firstly, perhaps the biggest minefield – grafting tomatoes onto potatoes.
Transplanting tomatoes onto potatoes was hailed as a revolutionary breakthrough, though in fact it is an old trick that was attempted back in the 1920s.
Tomatoes and potatoes belong to the same family, and their “pipes” inside are quite similar, making it indeed possible to create a super variant through grafting.
But the problem is that creating resource-intensive structures such as fruit and root crops requires a lot of energy for metabolism, and grafted plants need to meet the energy demands of two crops simultaneously, leaving limited energy to be diverted.
This means that the yield of these grafted plants is very low, quite different from what you see in the promotional pictures.
Moreover, the time of crop maturity varies, so harvesting potatoes requires killing tomatoes that have not yet matured.
Similarly, the promotional images also contain many misleading tricks.
I have seen roses processed into a sky blue color using Photoshop in some directories, and I have also come across fruits from trees that seem to bear an不可思议number of fruits.
Some merchants even “borrow” photos from my Instagram account and falsely claim them to be part of their test bases on online shopping websites.
These unscrupulous merchants' actions make you have to look at these promotional images with a skeptical eye, especially those from the internet.
If it looks too good to be true, it probably is.
Talking about blue roses, blue is relatively rare in the plant kingdom, so if you see new varieties of beautiful broom rashan, orchids, or cacti glowing with a blue light under a high concentration of what appears to be disinfectant, it is almost certainly due to dyeing or spraying with pesticide.
If suppliers explicitly state the situation, that’s fine, but they rarely do so.
The blue butterfly orchid will revert to its original white color, whereas cacti and box thorn coated with a reflective dye will quickly wilt.
Finally, I must reveal the bonsai set that promises to grow miniature trees, and there are even worse bonsai seeds than these sets.
Bonsai is not a plant.
This is a pruning technique reminiscent of ancient trees on distant mountains.
Kiosks are typically mature shrubs that are then shaped and guide new growth directions with wires.
I am aware that I will continue to be captivated by these horticultural tricks, which is one of the joys of experimentation.
But by avoiding these issues, you may have an opportunity to purchase an item that matches what the seller is showcasing.
Spanish quarantine measures: Do I need to self-isolate in Spain?
No, but if you return to Spain after 12:00 PM tonight, you must undergo home quarantine for 14 days.
UK Justice Secretary Humza Yousaf said: "We decided earlier this week not to enforce quarantine measures for those returning from Spain because data shows an improvement in the spread of the virus."
But clearly, the latest data warrants our concern, and we therefore have to overturn the original decision.
We know it may disappoint people. But we have consistently made clear that we will closely monitor outbreaks in different countries. If the virus reemerges in any country, we might ask them to remove themselves from the list of those not enforcing quarantine measures.”
Viruses remain rampant and deadly. Our top priority is to contain the virus, prevent its spread, and protect public health.
The government has not yet announced specific regulations for British citizens visiting Spain, but if you plan to visit Spain, you may want to check the rules for your area first.
Spanish Deputy Director of Emergency Health, Maria José Sierra, stated:
"There has been a major outbreak here. This may be the second wave. We need to keep an eye on the situation over the next few weeks."
Imam-ul-Haq of the Pakistan team continues to compete despite injuring himself during warm-up sessions.
The Pakistan team and the England team will face each other in three Test matches and three T20Is.
Pakistan cricket player Imam-ul-Haq was injured when hit in the left hand during a team warm-up match in a derby, and the team doctor announced that he is now fit to play.
The Pakistan Cricket Board (PCB) stated in a release, "The left-handed cricketer participated in a warm-up match before the game began on the second day."
On the first day of the tournament, when PCB White Team's Imam and PCB Green Team's Naseem Shah exchanged punches, Imam was hit in the left hand.
On Friday night, Imam was injured when his left hand was hit by a ball thrown by Shah and had to withdraw from the game, but he participated in the morning warm-up match and will return to the field on the third day.
Over 30 employees of a bank in Tamil Nadu have tested positive for COVID-19.
Officials from the government departments and a state-owned bank in Tirugurabair, Tamil Nadu, India, said that at least 38 employees of a major branch of the bank located in Tirugurabair have tested positive for Coronavirus.
Officials say they have suggested that customers who have visited the branch voluntarily participate in virus testing.
Previously, a senior employee of the branch had other complications and later died from COVID-19.
Officials stated that recently, they conducted a large-scale medical screening at the branch, which led to the discovery of these positive cases.
The branch leader said that they have already completed the disinfection, and the bank may resume operations tomorrow.
Company health officials are contacting customers who visited that bank.
Tamil Nadu is the second-most affected state in India in terms of confirmed cases, with Maharashtra reporting the highest number of infections. As of Saturday, Tamil Nadu had reported a total of 206,737 confirmed cases.
India's Prime Minister will attend the groundbreaking ceremony for a temple built on a disputed site.
New Delhi (AP) – A trustee overseeing temple construction said Indian Prime Minister Narendra Modi plans to attend next month the groundbreaking ceremony for a Hindu temple being built on a disputed site in northern India, where a mosque that dated back to the 16th century was destroyed by Hindu extremists in 1992.
The ceremony is scheduled for August 5th, when organizers consider it a significant day for Hindus, though it marks one year since India's parliament revoked the special autonomous status of the Muslim-majority Jammu and Kashmir state.
Modi’s supporters and opponents in India’s Hindu nationalist Bharatiya Janata Party (BJP) will not miss this symbolic day, when the party has long promised to take back Kashmir’s autonomy and build a temple dedicated to the Hindu deity Ram on the site of a mosque that predates the Mughal era, with believers claiming it is the birthplace of Ram. The site is located in Ayodhya, Uttar Pradesh.
The coronavirus continues to spread across India, where it has reportedly become the country with the third-highest number of infections globally. In light of this, the Nationalist Hindu group Vishwa Hindu Parishad, which has an alliance with the ruling Bharatiya Janata Party (BJP), has announced that the ceremony will be live-streamed on national television and that attendance at the event will be limited in both participants and audience members.
The organization's spokesperson, Vinod Bansal, said at a press conference on Saturday that the temple will become "a perpetual center for social harmony, national unity and integration, awakening of Hinduism."
Following the overwhelming victory of the Bharatiya Janata Party in the election, a century-long dispute over the site was resolved last year.
In November, the Supreme Court ruled in favor of the temple trust, ordering that five acres be allocated elsewhere for Muslim petitioners.
Hindu extremists have long claimed that Mughal Muslim invaders built a mosque on top of the existing temples in the ancient city of Ayodhya.
Violent communal incidents that followed rioting after the demolition of the mosque in December 1992 resulted in the deaths of approximately 2,000 people, of whom the majority were Muslims.
Meanwhile, the special court is still hearing the case of the mosque demolition.
A builder from Gujarat, India, home state of Prime Minister Modi, proposed building a 161-foot (49-meter) high tower of sandstone with five domes.
